data.source:
A content site rarely needs JavaScript on its public pages. Mortar renders on the server (brick loaders included), so you can send those pages as plain HTML and hydrate only the admin.

`createRender` from `@mortar-kit/core/render` does it. Use it as your server entry, and put `<ClientScripts />` in your document's head in place of the hydration script and client entry:

```tsx
// src/entry-server.tsx
import { createRender } from '@mortar-kit/core/render';
import manifest from 'virtual:solid-manifest';
import { Root } from './Root.tsx';

export const render = createRender({ manifest, app: () => <Root /> });
```

```tsx
// src/entry-client.tsx
import { hydrate } from '@solidjs/web';
import { Root } from './Root.tsx';

hydrate(() => <Root />, document);
```

```tsx
// src/Document.tsx (Root renders it around your App)
import { ClientScripts } from '@mortar-kit/core';

export default function Document(props) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <head>
        <meta charset="utf-8" />
        <ClientScripts />
      </head>
      <body>{props.children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

`hydrate` picks the pages that load JavaScript. The default is `['/admin']`, so the editor and its preview hydrate. It also takes path prefixes (matched by whole segments, in any case), `true`, `false`, or a function of the path. On every other page:

- the render runs under `<NoHydration>` with `noScripts`, so there are no hydration keys and no hydration data;
- `<ClientScripts>` renders nothing, so there's no client entry;
- the manifest gives stylesheets only, so the browser doesn't preload any JavaScript either;
- the page is always sent as one settled document, since streamed content needs a script.

When the plugin generates your entries, every page hydrates. Authoring the two entries above is what switches that off. `createRender` puts the request's CSP nonce on the scripts of hydrating pages.

The result: the example site's home page is about 1.3 kB of HTML plus two stylesheets, and no scripts.
